With all that talk of let it go, I don't have that frozen song playing in my head.

00:03.839 --> 00:06.780
I hope I haven't done that to you two now.

00:06.780 --> 00:13.900
Anyways, so building on that, I want to show you what I would describe as the evolution

00:13.900 --> 00:15.179
of workflows.

00:15.179 --> 00:17.940
Workflow is a very common word in this field.

00:17.940 --> 00:24.480
The workflow is about how do you go about organizing your activities around this mysterious,

00:24.480 --> 00:26.920
magical thing that is a coding agent.

00:26.920 --> 00:31.320
Workflow is really what Andrei Karpathy was getting at with that tweet.

00:31.320 --> 00:40.600
The optimal workflow with coding agents has changed consistently with what I just described

00:40.600 --> 00:43.679
with the different school of thought between the two years.

00:43.679 --> 00:49.399
Let me take you through what I think of as the six different workflows which is some

00:49.399 --> 00:54.799
one analogous to the eight stages that we covered yesterday. So in the 2025

00:54.799 --> 01:00.560
mindset there were perhaps three different levels of trusting and LLM.

01:00.560 --> 01:05.599
At the lowest level of trust where we began is micromanagement and this is

01:05.599 --> 01:10.920
where you would use an agent but you, when I see you, I mean me,

01:10.920 --> 01:14.200
this is definitely was my mode for most of last year.

01:14.200 --> 01:18.400
I would very much write very specific instructions

01:18.400 --> 01:21.560
in agents.md, approve all of the changes,

01:21.560 --> 01:24.040
frequently stop it running,

01:24.040 --> 01:27.560
go back rewrite agents.md and then run it again

01:27.560 --> 01:31.680
and then check what it's doing with these frequent resets.

01:31.680 --> 01:35.799
And that was where I began and where many people began

01:35.799 --> 01:39.040
using some of these agent coders.

01:39.040 --> 01:43.079
And then the next step was taking a bit of a step back,

01:43.079 --> 01:44.480
a bit more trust.

01:44.480 --> 01:46.120
Now, a lot of these programs,

01:46.120 --> 01:49.640
a lot of these coding agents have a planning mode.

01:49.640 --> 01:51.640
You can put them on plan mode.

01:51.640 --> 01:53.040
And when they're on plan mode,

01:53.040 --> 01:54.840
they produce documentation

01:54.840 --> 01:57.799
like sort of to-do lists of what's going to happen.

01:57.799 --> 02:00.359
And you're working together on figuring out

02:00.359 --> 02:02.319
the plan for what's to be done.

02:02.319 --> 02:05.000
And then when plan mode is done, you say, okay,

02:05.000 --> 02:08.479
let's do this, and you put it onto execution mode.

02:08.479 --> 02:11.680
And so that idea of planning first, and then executing,

02:11.680 --> 02:13.840
and typically you execute in phases.

02:13.840 --> 02:17.360
And for each phase, I would have it go off and build something, then I would

02:17.360 --> 02:22.319
do a code review and make sure it's not making a mountain out of a molehill as they often like to do

02:22.319 --> 02:27.039
and then after that I would have it test and I would come back and review the test results, do some

02:27.039 --> 02:32.879
testing myself and then mark it complete and move on to the next phase. So that is this kind of plan

02:32.879 --> 02:39.680
execute review test giving it a little bit more wiggle room, a little bit more rope, but generally

02:39.680 --> 02:45.680
still very much on top of things. And then the next phase is about spec-driven

02:45.680 --> 02:51.080
development, SDD, where you are really specifying what needs to be done and

02:51.080 --> 02:56.039
then letting it go and do it and it's been specified there are even some some

02:56.039 --> 03:01.520
sort of spec languages when you can describe the spec in this very precise way.

03:01.520 --> 03:07.000
And really the other term for this is people talk about trust but verify.

03:07.000 --> 03:11.719
Trust the system, you come back to verify everything's doing what you wanted it to do.

03:11.719 --> 03:17.039
But you're not doing the same kind of code review or detail testing until the end.

03:17.039 --> 03:25.159
So it's very much more of a step backwards and that the trust but verify mindset is something that has become

03:25.159 --> 03:26.439
increasingly common.

03:26.439 --> 03:29.960
So these are all sort of consistent with the 2025 approaches.

03:29.960 --> 03:34.919
Okay, now onto the 2026 mindset starting with YOLO.

03:34.919 --> 03:38.120
YOLO, of course, has been around for a while.

03:38.120 --> 03:42.840
Most of 2025, it's not a new thing, but I would argue that it's only recently that people

03:42.840 --> 03:44.979
are actually using this beyond

03:44.979 --> 03:46.919
just for hobby projects.

03:46.919 --> 03:48.479
People are now using this for real.

03:48.479 --> 03:52.080
That's why I'm putting it in the 2026 mindset category.

03:52.080 --> 03:55.180
YOLO, of course, is when you say, "You know what?

03:55.180 --> 03:58.039
I don't need to approve anything."

03:58.039 --> 04:01.240
The coding agent can do whatever it wants.

04:01.240 --> 04:02.680
No permissions needed.

04:02.680 --> 04:04.719
So it's like, "Trust but verify.

04:04.719 --> 04:06.919
Take it to a whole new level. You could just set

04:06.919 --> 04:11.919
this thing going and go off and have dinner and come back later and see what it did. It's

04:11.919 --> 04:16.959
never going to stop and ask, do you want to do this or not? That is the idea behind Yolo,

04:16.959 --> 04:19.360
a whole different level of trust.

04:19.360 --> 04:25.399
And next up are things called Ralph Loops. Ralph Loops were invented by the Australian

04:25.399 --> 04:28.399
developer, Luminary, Jeffrey Huntley, and it's named after the Australian developer, Luminary, Geoffrey Huntley,

04:28.399 --> 04:32.100
and it's named after the Simpsons character, Ralph Wigam.

04:32.100 --> 04:34.740
And then so the full name is Ralph Wigam-loops.

04:34.740 --> 04:37.279
And he's recorded in Simpsons,

04:37.279 --> 04:40.759
this is like a sort of naive, optimistic character,

04:40.759 --> 04:43.879
and that is true to this design pattern.

04:43.879 --> 04:44.920
And here's the idea.

04:44.920 --> 04:49.300
So you know that already an agent is something that calls

04:49.300 --> 04:52.240
LLM in a loop to achieve a goal and it calls

04:52.240 --> 04:55.839
it multiple times until it thinks it's used its tools enough and it gives some sort of

04:55.839 --> 05:01.279
an output. Well the idea of Ralph Loops is saying, well hang on a minute, can we put

05:01.279 --> 05:06.199
this entire process with this little loop to achieve an output? Wrap that in another

05:06.199 --> 05:12.879
bigger loop. We will call this agent, we'll have it do its thing, use its tools, make some output.

05:12.879 --> 05:16.300
And then we'll do a test to say, has this done enough?

05:16.300 --> 05:19.040
Is this gone as far as it can go?

05:19.040 --> 05:21.120
Is this as good as it can be?

05:21.120 --> 05:28.199
And if not, make some feedback, think about what the gaps are, add all of that to the objectives,

05:28.199 --> 05:32.360
and then just call the whole thing again, and have it go off again and do its loop and

05:32.360 --> 05:34.220
come back, and then get some feedback and just simply go off again and do its loop and come back and then get some

05:34.220 --> 05:40.379
feedback and just simply call it again and just keep going. Keep going up to

05:40.379 --> 05:46.560
some large number of times like 10 full times of doing this inner loop.

05:46.560 --> 05:53.879
10 big loops of lots of little loops. That is a Ralph Wigam loop and that idea

05:53.879 --> 05:57.680
so I said with YOLO that you could leave something running over dinner

05:57.680 --> 06:01.920
with Ralph Loops, you could leave something running overnight and come back the next day

06:01.920 --> 06:10.240
and it's just done tons. That's the idea of Ralph Loops and that is another whole new level of trust.

06:10.240 --> 06:20.519
And to make this real for you, this of course is what I did with the second example yesterday with the much more impressive first person shooter game.

06:20.519 --> 06:23.720
It was just the same, the same prompt as before,

06:23.720 --> 06:26.379
but I put it in one of these Ralph Loops,

06:26.379 --> 06:29.560
and I just left it going, and I just chugged away,

06:29.560 --> 06:32.879
and I came back a lot later, and that's what I had,

06:32.879 --> 06:35.040
and it was what we call one shot,

06:35.040 --> 06:39.240
and I didn't keep coming back and giving it more feedback

06:39.240 --> 06:41.139
and saying I'd like better graphics. I'd like this

06:41.139 --> 06:41.699
I'd like this

06:41.699 --> 06:48.240
I just left it doing its own thing and it was an L M that decided to come up with the feedback and to run it again

06:48.240 --> 06:54.079
And it kept going ten times and that was the output and so you know that that's what it does

06:54.079 --> 06:55.680
And when I say Ralph Loops here

06:55.680 --> 07:07.600
It's a bit of a point in time. There's a lot of hype about these right now, and I really mean this as an overall category. It's Ralph Loops and things like that. Ways to have a much longer running task. Not

07:07.600 --> 07:12.120
something, a YOLO is perhaps something that runs for an hour. A Ralph Loops is

07:12.120 --> 07:17.199
about bigger ways to run this kind of agentic coding in a way that's not

07:17.199 --> 07:21.879
measured in minutes but it's measured in multiple hours. It's perhaps something

07:21.879 --> 07:26.459
that runs overnight. It's a long running process to run something

07:26.459 --> 07:31.620
completely trusted in a way that you will come back later and see what it's made with

07:31.620 --> 07:34.160
its own internal feedback mechanism.

07:34.160 --> 07:36.019
That's the bigger concept here.

07:36.019 --> 07:42.639
And that leads to the sixth approach, which is then about multi-agents.

07:42.639 --> 07:45.519
This is about, it's sort of an extension of Ralph Loops.

07:45.519 --> 07:47.040
In Ralph Loops, there is another

07:47.040 --> 07:52.800
LLM that's doing some of the feedback potentially. But this is about where you have many different

07:52.800 --> 07:58.240
agents that are working with different roles, perhaps there are testing agents, feedback agents.

07:58.240 --> 08:02.160
There are agents organized into different categories and there are manager agents as we discussed

