and you talk about orchestration if you are doing the kinds of setups that again that we

08:15.360 --> 08:22.240
discussed yesterday with the eighth stage. But that kind of workflow, that is really the ultimate,

08:22.959 --> 08:33.039
that is what people at the forefront of this are doing now in 2026. So this is how I've seen the workflows evolve and of course this is pretty

08:33.039 --> 08:39.279
similar to Steve Yaggy's different stages of these coding agents that we talked about yesterday.

08:40.080 --> 08:47.759
And I would say that the question that I'm sure is on your mind is which approach is right and

08:47.759 --> 08:57.000
you know the classic answer to these things is that it's not like there's one approach that's right, it's that different approaches are right for different tasks.

08:57.000 --> 09:00.039
And I would divide it into two categories.

09:00.039 --> 09:05.720
If you have things that are mission critical, where you're working on enterprise software

09:05.720 --> 09:10.799
on or on like a commercial SAS platform, or you're working with a large code base, or

09:10.799 --> 09:15.240
you're working with highly innovative code, like you're trying to generate something

09:15.240 --> 09:18.039
that is going to maybe use MCP servers

09:18.039 --> 09:18.879
or something like that.

09:18.879 --> 09:21.879
You're at the forefront of what you're trying to build.

09:21.879 --> 09:24.240
Then to me, that lends itself

09:24.240 --> 09:27.000
to those first three approaches.

09:27.000 --> 09:29.200
You're somewhere on that curve.

09:29.200 --> 09:32.879
And so that would be one category.

09:32.879 --> 09:38.720
But by contrast, if you were building an MVP, like a prototype, a pilot, or you're

09:38.720 --> 09:44.240
